  "articleBody": "Staining your cabin interior can transform the space, but it's easy to make a few missteps along the way. Here are some common mistakes and how to steer clear of them:\n\n1.  **Skipping the Test Patch:** Don't just dive in! Always test your stain on an inconspicuous area first. Wood species and previous treatments can drastically change the final color. A little test saves a lot of heartache.\n2.  **Poor Sanding or Dust Removal:** If your surface isn't properly sanded and free of dust, your stain will look uneven and splotchy. Take the time to prep the surface thoroughly – it really makes a difference.\n3.  **Applying Too Much Stain:** More isn't always better. Applying too much stain can lead to a sticky, uneven finish that won't dry properly. Apply in thin, even coats and wipe away any excess promptly.\n4.  **Ignoring Wood Type Differences:** Remember that different woods absorb stain differently. What works for pine might not for cedar. Adjust your approach based on the specific wood in your cabin.\n5.  **Not Following Manufacturer Instructions:** Every stain product has specific application and drying guidelines. Deviating from these can lead to poor results, like long drying times or an inconsistent finish. Always read the label! If you're tackling a big project or have really varied wood surfaces, calling in an expert can save you from these common pitfalls.",
